WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.560
Defenders use the Cyber Kill Chain for more than incident labels.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.360
It can organize timelines, map detection coverage, compare preventive controls,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.700
structure tabletop exercises, support threat intelligence reporting,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.720
and explain an incident to leaders in plain language.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.640
Its usefulness depends on flexibility.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.400
Modern activity may be nonlinear, cloud-native, identity-driven, automated,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.400
insider-enabled, or hidden inside legitimate tools.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.945
Some stages may be compressed.

00:00:23.945 --> 00:00:25.000
Some may repeat.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.282
Some may not be visible with the telemetry available.

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:29.880
Common mistakes include treating the model as a rigid checklist,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.890
assuming every incident must map cleanly,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.135
equating one alert with full understanding,

00:00:34.135 --> 00:00:36.930
or focusing only on malware while ignoring identity,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.320
cloud, SaaS, and business-process evidence.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.600
The model works best when it improves questions.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.566
What have we observed?

00:00:42.566 --> 00:00:43.600
What are we inferring?

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.780
What confidence do we have?

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:45.912
Which controls helped?

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:46.933
Which gaps mattered?

00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:47.960
What should change?

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.720
Use it alongside incident response practice, threat modeling, telemetry engineering,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.200
control validation, intelligence analysis, and business context.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.355
A good Kill Chain discussion should leave the team with clearer evidence,

00:00:58.355 --> 00:01:00.480
better priorities, and defensible next steps.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.000
A healthy use of the model also invites disagreement.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.880
If one analyst maps behavior to exploitation and another sees it as delivery evidence,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.100
the team should compare facts rather than force consensus too early.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.190
That discussion often reveals missing telemetry or assumptions that deserve documentation.
